  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Make plans for tomorrow, the weekend and the years ahead, and order politely with ningependa.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Utaenda wapi wikendi?", "base": "Where will you go at the weekend?" },
      { "target": "Tutakutana saa nane.", "base": "We will meet at two o’clock." },
      { "target": "Nitarudi wiki ijayo.", "base": "I will come back next week." },
      { "target": "Una mpango gani?", "base": "What’s your plan?" },
      { "target": "Ningependa kahawa, tafadhali.", "base": "I would like coffee, please." },
      { "target": "Siku moja nitakuwa mwalimu.", "base": "One day I will be a teacher." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "ta-everyone",
        "title": "The future for everyone",
        "body": [
          "**-ta-** works with every person: **nitaenda** (I will go), **utaenda** (you will go), **atakuja** (she will come), **tutakutana** (we will meet), **watarudi** (they will come back).",
          "Short verbs keep **ku-** in the future: **nitakula** (I will eat), **atakuja** (she will come)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "would-like",
        "title": "Would: -nge-",
        "body": [
          "**-nge-** means “would”: **ningependa** (I would like), **ungependa?** (would you like?). It is the polite way to ask for something.",
          "**Ningependa kahawa** is softer than **Nataka kahawa** (I want coffee), and is what you’ll hear in a hotel or office."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Mungu akipenda",
        "body": [
          "Plans are often finished with **Mungu akipenda** or **Inshallah** (“if God wills”): **Tutaonana kesho, Mungu akipenda.** It is said by Christians and Muslims alike."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
